            <header class="lesson-header"><span class="lesson-number">Lesson 7</span><h1>Singleton Pattern</h1><div class="lesson-meta"><span class="lesson-duration"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"/></svg>25 min</span><span class="lesson-level advanced">Advanced</span></div></header>
            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Intent</h2>
                    <p>Ensure a class has only one instance and provide a global point of access to it.</p>
                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/></svg><span>Category</span></div>
                        <p>Creational Pattern</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Thread-Safe Singleton (Lazy)</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">public sealed class Logger
{
    private static readonly Lazy&lt;Logger&gt; _instance = 
        new(() =&gt; new Logger());

    public static Logger Instance =&gt; _instance.Value;

    private Logger() 
    {
        // Private constructor prevents external instantiation
    }

    public void Log(string message)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"[{DateTime.Now}] {message}");
    }
}

// Usage
Logger.Instance.Log("Application started");</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Alternative: Static Initialization</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">public sealed class Configuration
{
    // Eager initialization - thread-safe by CLR guarantee
    private static readonly Configuration _instance = new();

    public static Configuration Instance =&gt; _instance;

    private Configuration()
    {
        // Load configuration
    }

    public string ConnectionString { get; private set; }
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Modern Approach: Use DI Container</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Register as singleton in DI
builder.Services.AddSingleton&lt;ILogger, Logger&gt;();
builder.Services.AddSingleton&lt;IConfiguration&gt;(config);

// Inject where needed - no static access!
public class OrderService
{
    private readonly ILogger _logger;

    public OrderService(ILogger logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }
}</code></pre>
                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/></svg><span>Best Practice</span></div>
                        <p>In modern .NET, prefer using the DI container's singleton lifetime over implementing the pattern manually.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>When to Use</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Configuration settings</li>
                        <li>Logging services</li>
                        <li>Database connection pools</li>
                        <li>Caches</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Singleton ensures only one instance exists.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use Lazy&lt;T&gt; for thread-safe lazy initialization.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Prefer DI container's singleton registration.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Be cautious—singletons can make testing harder.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>
            </div>
